##
    Reaching a type declared inside another the wrong way.

    This program does not compile. Nesting says where a type belongs, and that answer is what
    decides how it is named: from outside, the container is written first, and a nested type
    says who may name it exactly as any other member does. samples/nesting.pc holds that side.

    Two mistakes, and they are opposites. A bare name reaches a nested type only from inside the
    container, so writing one from outside finds nothing — the name was never loose in the
    program. And a nested type that says nothing about its reach is the container's own, so
    naming it from elsewhere is refused however correctly it is written.

    Nothing here is run, so nothing reads what it declares and nothing keeps what it
    works out. That is the shape of every negative, and not the lesson of this one.
# ignore PC0409 in file
# ignore PC0410 in file
##

shared model Program

    function Main()
        # A bare name never reached across the program. Tree is what holds Node.
        Node loose = new Node(1);

        Console.WriteLine(loose.value);

        # Written correctly, and still refused: Hidden is Tree's own.
        Tree.Hidden kept = new Tree.Hidden(2);

        Console.WriteLine(kept.value);
    end function
end model

model Tree

    public model Node
        public integer value;

        public function Node(integer held)
            this.value = held;
        end function
    end model

    # No word, so this one belongs to Tree and to nothing else.
    model Hidden
        public integer value;

        public function Hidden(integer held)
            this.value = held;
        end function
    end model
end model
